<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <RootNamespace>Ruledger</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>False</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1605;CS1591</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    What Ruledger is: the walk, the test design and the diff, for a host to call. The command
    line is one such host and lives in Ruledger.Cli; a host that shows a diff its own way —
    on an application's own window, say — asks this for it rather than reading the lines a
    command prints, which would be a second account of what changed.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>Ruledger</PackageId>
    <Version>1.3.0</Version>
    <Authors>Reny</Authors>
    <Description>Walks a Rulealize rule set and writes down, for every state it visits, everything that can be observed there: which inputs are legal, whether it is final and with what result, and where each legal input leads. Applying that test design to a later version of the rule set is the diff, and the choices a person made are carried across it.</Description>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/reny-develop/Ruledger</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The runtime. Walking the states is its two calls in the same order every host makes them,
    and the expected values are what it answers — there is no second implementation of the
    rules here to disagree with it.

    Reading the document statically is this project's own, because nothing published reports
    which state a guard reads. Where that costs something is written down in
    ObservationScan.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Rulealize" Version="0.11.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    So that the measurement can walk a rule set the wrong way on purpose. Showing that
    collapsing is needed means walking without it, and that is not a thing to offer a caller:
    a walk told to compare whole states is a walk that does not come back.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Ruledger.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Ruledger.Verify" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
